The vertex of a parabola is the point at which the slope of the tangent line is zero. To find the slope of the tangent line to the curve at any point $(t, f(t)),$ we need to take the derivative of $y$ with respect to $x.$
Using the chain rule, we have: \begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= \frac{dy}{dt} \cdot \frac{dt}{dx} \\
&= \frac{d(2t-1)}{dt} \cdot \frac{d(t^2+t)}{dt} \\
&= 2 \cdot (2t+1) \\
&= 4t+2
\end{align*}
Setting this equal to zero gives us the $t$-coordinate of the vertex: $4t+2=0 \Rightarrow t=-\frac{1}{2}.$
Substituting $t=-\frac{1}{2}$ into the parametric equations for $x$ and $y,$ we get the coordinates of the vertex: \begin{align*}
x &= \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)^2 + \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) = \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{2} = -\frac{1}{4} \\
y &= 2\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) - 1 = -1 - 1 = -2
\end{align*}
Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is $\boxed{\left(-\frac{1}{4},-2\right)}.$

